<div class="biography-reading" data-page="about"><div id="article" class="shell"><article aria-labelledby="article-title"><div class="article-top"><div class="eyebrow"><span>About</span><span class="article-number">01 / 04</span></div><h1 id="article-title">About Erik Norris</h1><div class="byline"><span>By Erik Norris</span><span>6 minute read</span></div><p class="lede" data-source-paragraph="0">I&#x27;m an engineer. I like doing good work on hard problems, and I love the physics and figuring things out. I like starting with a concept, when we&#x27;re still working out what it could be and how it might work. I want to understand the system well enough to test something meaningful, then use what we learn to make the compromises without losing the intent. Doing that work early gives us more room to get the important decisions right and carry them through to something we can build and use.</p></div>
<div class="reading-layout"><aside class="reading-sidebar" aria-label="Reading guide"><div class="sidebar-inner"><span class="small-label">In this account</span><nav aria-label="Article sections"><a href="#section-1">Engineering practice</a><a href="#section-4">Family and the coast</a><a href="#section-9">The repair shop</a><a href="#section-12">Product engineering</a><a href="#section-16">Tools and records</a><a href="#section-21">Looking closely</a><a href="#section-23">The next work</a></nav><div class="sidebar-connected"><span class="small-label">Connected reading</span><a href="https://moreplay.com/entries/family-and-the-coast/"><span>Family and coast</span><small>Moreplay</small></a><a href="https://moreplay.com/entries/the-repair-shop/"><span>The repair shop</span><small>Moreplay</small></a><a href="https://mechanistic.com/tools-and-working-records/"><span>Tools and records</span><small>Mechanistic</small></a></div></div></aside><div class="prose"><h2 id="section-1">Engineering practice</h2><p data-source-paragraph="1">I run Mechanistic, an independent engineering practice. My work has ranged from workstations and professional audio equipment to home-cinema systems, wearables, connected lighting and food automation. I also design and build software for my own research and engineering work.</p><p data-source-paragraph="2">At Hyphen, I led mechanical design and systems architecture for the Augmented Makeline. Food handling, bowl movement, refrigeration, controls and cabinet packaging had to work together in equipment people could operate, clean and maintain in a working kitchen. The interfaces mattered as much as the individual mechanisms: where things fit, how they connected and how someone would reach them for service.</p><p data-source-paragraph="3" class="story-links"><a href="https://eriknorris.com/projects/" class="" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" title="Opens the live website in a new tab">Explore the work</a> · <a href="https://eriknorris.com/resume/" class="">Resume</a> · <a href="https://eriknorris.com/contact/" class="" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" title="Opens the live website in a new tab">Get in touch</a></p><h2 id="section-4">Family and the coast</h2><p data-source-paragraph="4">I grew up in British Columbia, around people who built and repaired things. Dad built custom homes and compounds. Near Egmont, his crew brought logs into a bay, hauled them up the hill by cable and milled them onsite. Elevated plank walkways connected the cabins. We tied the crew boat to the log boom and crossed the logs to get ashore. I went along and helped.</p><p data-source-paragraph="5">At about twelve I wanted a motorcycle. Mom said I could have it if I paid for it myself, so I split wood and sold it by the cord. Dad and my uncles collected the XL175 from Vancouver in the truck. Back at the farm, I rebuilt its carburetor, replaced its camshaft and rode it.</p><p data-source-paragraph="6">Mom made things too: paintings, poems, knitting, pieces for her grandchildren. She wrote about learning to knit with her mother and the pleasure of shared work. In her poem <em>Silver Buttons</em>, ordinary buttons carried stories about the people who had worn them.</p><p data-source-paragraph="7">I&#x27;m bringing the family history together with the photographs, Mom&#x27;s writing and the scientific papers my biological father gave me during a visit to Sandia. There is a lot of life in that material: the coast, the places we moved between, work, family and things people made because they enjoyed making them.</p><p data-source-paragraph="8" class="story-links"><a href="https://moreplay.com/entries/family-and-the-coast/" class="">Family and the coast · Moreplay →</a></p><h2 id="section-9">The repair shop</h2><p data-source-paragraph="9">In my teens I moved to Palo Alto. Automotive work continued alongside electronics and design. I managed Alice&#x27;s Garage in Woodside and ran my own repair shop, including racing preparation and fabrication. I also built a loaner fleet. A customer could bring in a Honda for brakes and an oil change and leave in a &#x27;75 Malibu wagon for the day. They loved it.</p><p data-source-paragraph="10">The work involved the whole situation: finding the fault, making the repair, getting parts and keeping the customer moving. Computer-controlled engines brought mechanical behavior and electrical diagnosis together. That experience still affects what I notice in a product: whether you can reach the part, diagnose a fault and repair it without turning a small problem into a large job.</p><p data-source-paragraph="11" class="story-links"><a href="https://moreplay.com/entries/the-repair-shop/" class="">The repair shop and its tools · Moreplay →</a></p><h2 id="section-12">Product engineering</h2><p data-source-paragraph="12">At EP Technologies I assembled and reworked electronics and learned component-level troubleshooting. At Silicon Graphics I worked on the mechanical and thermal problems of workstations. With industrial-design teams, and through Mechanistic, I developed complex surfaces, mechanical packaging and designs for manufacturing.</p><p data-source-paragraph="13">On Kaleidescape&#x27;s Cinema One, I developed plastic skins over a sheet-metal base to achieve the intended aluminum-like form within the product&#x27;s cost, manufacturing, grounding and surface-temperature constraints. The rectangular mass and floating front bezel mattered, along with how the product would be built. I owned the mechanical design through production bring-up. I loved the way it turned out.</p><p data-source-paragraph="14">On Digidesign&#x27;s C|24, I owned the mechanical engineering and industrial design. One small part of that was the headphone jack. Its position left the plug vulnerable to being struck, and replacement was too invasive. Recessing it created another problem: real headphone plugs were awkward to use in the first mock-up. Working with Arndt Hufenbach and Manufacturing, I designed the field-replaceable assembly that shipped. <a href="https://eriknorris.com/projects/c24/" class="" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" title="Opens the live website in a new tab">Read the C|24 account.</a></p><p data-source-paragraph="15">At Avegant, I owned Glyph&#x27;s headband test plan and worked with colleagues on prototypes, testing and analysis. Comparing complete units with selected components removed helped us understand their contributions to fit and force. At Noon, I used prototype tests and sample-level results to distinguish glass damage, coating problems, retention, rattle and functional failures. In both cases, the tests helped us understand what needed to change in the design. <a href="https://eriknorris.com/projects/avegant-glyph/" class="" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" title="Opens the live website in a new tab">Read the Glyph account.</a></p><h2 id="section-16">Tools and records</h2><p data-source-paragraph="16">Independent practice also made the engineering record part of my job. I needed to integrate with a client&#x27;s team, manage revisions and leave usable CAD deliverables. What changed, why it changed, and which version someone should build are practical engineering questions.</p><p data-source-paragraph="17">I&#x27;ve been collecting tools and ways of working for most of my career. As a mechanic, I looked forward to the Snap-on truck. Good tools were exciting and expensive. Learning software and AI has given me another way to make tools to my specifications, for my work.</p><p data-source-paragraph="18">It has also involved finding out what doesn&#x27;t work yet. I could get useful work done, then spend the next session trying to recover where we had left off. I wanted to return to a project, understand its state and keep going.</p><p data-source-paragraph="19">I design, build and operate EN-OS, a local system that keeps engineering sources, decisions and ongoing work usable across AI assistants. I own its architecture, software development, testing and operation. It also helps me return to older work: recover photographs and correspondence, compare accounts and explain why a decision was made. This portfolio and the family-history collection both draw on that work. <a href="https://eriknorris.com/colophon/en-os/" class="" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" title="Opens the live website in a new tab">How the engineering record is built.</a></p><p data-source-paragraph="20" class="story-links"><a href="https://mechanistic.com/tools-and-working-records/" class="">Tools, records and the work between sessions · Mechanistic →</a></p><h2 id="section-21">Looking closely</h2><p data-source-paragraph="21">I&#x27;ve always been an avid photographer, and I use photography to communicate technical work. A photograph can show an assembly, a test setup or a detail that is difficult to explain on its own. Looking closely and finding a useful way to show someone else what I&#x27;m seeing are part of the job, and part of the enjoyment.</p><p data-source-paragraph="22">As a kid, I spent hours in libraries pulling microfiche. That experience came back when I was thinking about this site: scan quickly, find something interesting, hold a frame still and read. That&#x27;s the kind of discovery I want the portfolio to support. A photograph or a design decision should give you somewhere worth following.</p><h2 id="section-23">The next work</h2><p data-source-paragraph="23">I&#x27;m looking for a senior individual contributor role with responsibility for product architecture, technical strategy and development methods, and room to carry decisions through design, testing and manufacturing.</p><p data-source-paragraph="24" class="story-links"><a href="https://eriknorris.com/resume/" class="">Read my resume</a> or <a href="https://eriknorris.com/contact/" class="" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" title="Opens the live website in a new tab">get in touch</a>.</p><div class="end-mark" aria-hidden="true">EN</div></div></div>
